WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
I asked my class which fruit they

2
00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:05.270
like most, and drew a chart.

3
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.730
It looked like oranges won by a mile!

4
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.840
Then Musa said: Miss, your

5
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.620
bars don't start at zero.

6
00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.170
Today: draw charts from your data,

7
00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:19.390
and check that they're fair.

8
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.580
A pictogram uses little pictures.

9
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.140
Each picture stands for some

10
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.660
votes: one picture, two pupils.

11
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.650
Always write what one picture means.

12
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.500
A bar chart uses bars.

13
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.560
The taller the bar, the more votes.

14
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.120
Every bar has the same width, a label

15
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.220
underneath, and starts at zero.

16
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.590
To read a bar chart, find the

17
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.270
tallest bar and the shortest.

18
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.860
Then compare: oranges got five

19
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.060
more votes than bananas.

20
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:53.860
Quick check!

21
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.500
In a pictogram, one orange

22
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.160
picture means two pupils.

23
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.110
There are four pictures.

24
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.220
How many pupils is that?

25
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:03.790
Eight.

26
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.810
Four pictures, two pupils each:

27
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.890
two, four, six, eight.

28
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.680
Charts must be fair.

29
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.920
Bars start at zero, or a small

30
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.140
difference looks huge.

31
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.810
And every answer gets its

32
00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.410
bar, even the small ones.

33
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.360
Look for odd values too.

34
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.470
If a soil reading jumps to zero and

35
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.370
straight back, something went wrong.

36
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.600
On a chart, it jumps out.

37
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.740
In the app, a week of soil

38
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.160
readings from the school garden.

39
00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.870
Find the red dots: the

40
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.470
readings that can't be right.

41
00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.350
Tap “Leave them out”, and see

42
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.570
the lowest reading change.

43
00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:46.650
Your turn.

44
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.410
Which of these charts is fair?

45
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.330
The one with bars from zero,

46
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.910
and every answer shown.

47
00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.530
Remember: a pictogram says

48
00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.130
what one picture means.

49
00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.690
The tallest bar has the most.

50
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.600
And a fair chart starts at zero.

51
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.590
Try it tomorrow: turn your tally from

52
00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.950
the first lesson into a bar chart.

53
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.640
Give it a title and labels.

54
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.390
And start the bars at zero!
